<h2>A short marriage and a shorter prosecution</h2>
<p>The marriage was solemnised in February 2022; the parties separated by January 2023; and FIR No. 0412/2024 was registered at PS Alipur on 28 June 2024 on the wife\'s complaint under Sections 498A, 406 and 34 IPC — provisions that now correspond to Sections 85, 316(2) and 3(5) of the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita. The petitioners were the former husband and four of his relatives. No children were born of the marriage, and no chargesheet had been filed when the quashing petition was heard.</p>

<h2>The counselling cell as the engine of settlement</h2>
<p>What gives the case its practical interest is where the settlement was built: not in a private negotiation, but before the counselling cell attached to the Family Court, on 17 August 2024. The terms were concrete — Rs 2,45,000 in instalments, return of the complainant\'s articles, and her cooperation in quashing the FIR. By the time the matter reached Justice Sachin Datta, Rs 1.6 lakh stood paid, and the remaining Rs 85,000 was handed over by bank draft in the courtroom itself, with the complainant confirming receipt. A mutual consent divorce had been decreed on 20 March 2025. The complainant appeared, affirmed that the settlement was voluntary, and filed an affidavit of no objection.</p>
<div class="flow">
<div class="fstep"><strong>June 2024</strong> — FIR registered at PS Alipur.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>August 2024</strong> — Settlement recorded before the Family Court counselling cell.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>March 2025</strong> — Mutual consent divorce decreed.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>October 2026</strong> — Balance paid in court; FIR and all proceedings quashed.</div>
</div>

<h2>The legal frame</h2>
<p>Section 498A is non-compoundable, and the Court did not pretend otherwise. Its reasoning travelled the established path: the inherent power to quash is separate from, and unconstrained by, the statutory scheme of compounding. Drawing on <em>Gian Singh</em> (2012), <em>Narinder Singh</em> (2014) and <em>Jitendra Raghuvanshi</em> (2013), the Court asked whether the dispute was private and matrimonial in character, whether the settlement was voluntary, and whether any public interest demanded the prosecution continue. The offences alleged did not belong to the category of serious crimes such as murder, rape or dacoity; the parties had divorced; the money had been paid in full; and the complainant\'s consent was unambiguous. On those facts, conviction was a remote prospect and continuation of the proceedings futile. The FIR and everything emanating from it were quashed as against all five petitioners.</p>

<h2>Practice notes</h2>
<div class="check">
<ul>
<li>Record settlement terms before an institutional forum — a counselling cell, mediation centre or Lok Adalat — rather than on plain paper; the provenance of the settlement is itself evidence of voluntariness.</li>
<li>Complete, or substantially complete, the payments before the quashing hearing; the balance handed over in court leaves the judge nothing to doubt.</li>
<li>Bring the complainant to court. An affidavit helps; her presence and unequivocal statement help more.</li>
<li>Where the investigation has not reached chargesheet, say so — the futility argument is at its strongest before the State has invested in a final report.</li>
</ul>
</div>
<p>The order also carries a quiet message for complainants: a settlement reached through the Family Court\'s own machinery, honoured to the last instalment, is a reliable way to convert a matrimonial prosecution into a clean, final closure for both sides.</p>
